(The four ways to read a wait status, and the two kinds of death a child
 process can suffer.

 A wait status is one packed number: the low seven bits hold the signal that
 killed the process and are zero when it exited on its own, and the exit code
 lives in the next eight bits up. Only one of those halves means anything for
 any one status, which is what crashed and 'exited normally' are for - ask
 which death it was, then read the half that applies.

 The literal statuses below are what the kernel would have written. The last
 two sections check that claim against real forked children, so the demo
 tests the decoding against the kernel rather than against itself.

 This demo is the library's test - its output is compared byte for byte
 against process_demo.expected. A boolean prints as 1 or 0.

   vox process.vox --shared -o libprocess.so
   vox process_demo.vox --link process -o process_demo
   ./process_demo)

see process version "0.1" from "./libprocess.lib".

(A program that ran to the end and returned 7. The signal half is zero, so
 the exit code is the half worth reading.)
a number called 'a clean exit' is 1792.
Print "exit code: {'exit code of' of 'a clean exit'}".
Print "signal: {'signal of' of 'a clean exit'}".
Print "crashed: {crashed of 'a clean exit'}".
Print "exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'a clean exit'}".

(Success is zero all the way through, which is why "did it exit normally" has
 to be asked separately from "is the exit code zero".)
a number called 'a successful run' is 0.
Print "exit code: {'exit code of' of 'a successful run'}".
Print "signal: {'signal of' of 'a successful run'}".
Print "crashed: {crashed of 'a successful run'}".
Print "exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'a successful run'}".

(The largest exit code a program can return, 255, sitting in the high byte.)
a number called 'the largest exit code' is 65280.
Print "exit code: {'exit code of' of 'the largest exit code'}".
Print "exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'the largest exit code'}".

(Killed by signal 11, a segmentation fault. The exit code half is meaningless
 now - it reads as 0 whatever the program would have returned.)
a number called 'a segfault' is 11.
Print "exit code: {'exit code of' of 'a segfault'}".
Print "signal: {'signal of' of 'a segfault'}".
Print "crashed: {crashed of 'a segfault'}".
Print "exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'a segfault'}".

(The same death with a core dumped, which sets bit 128 alongside the signal.
 'signal of' masks that bit off and still answers 11, not 139.)
a number called 'a segfault with a core dumped' is 139.
Print "signal: {'signal of' of 'a segfault with a core dumped'}".
Print "crashed: {crashed of 'a segfault with a core dumped'}".
Print "exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'a segfault with a core dumped'}".

(Before any successful reap, `the reaped status` is -1 - a sentinel no real
 status can take. Decode it anyway and you get an answer, and the answer is
 nonsense: 127 is not a signal any child was killed by. Test for -1 before
 handing a status to any of these.)
a number called 'never reaped' is -1.
Print "exit code: {'exit code of' of 'never reaped'}".
Print "signal: {'signal of' of 'never reaped'}".
Print "crashed: {crashed of 'never reaped'}".
Print "exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'never reaped'}".

(A real child now, exiting with a code of its own choosing.)
a number called 'the well behaved child' is fork the process.
If 'the well behaved child' is 0 then,
    Exit 7.
If 'the well behaved child' is greater than 0 then,
    a number called 'the reaped child' is reap any child process,
    a number called 'how the child ended' is the reaped status,
    Print "a real exit code: {'exit code of' of 'how the child ended'}",
    Print "a real signal: {'signal of' of 'how the child ended'}",
    Print "really crashed: {crashed of 'how the child ended'}",
    Print "really exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'how the child ended'}".

(And a real child killed outright. SIGKILL cannot be caught or ignored, so the
 child dies wherever it has got to and the status is the same on every run.)
a number called 'the doomed child' is fork the process.
If 'the doomed child' is 0 then,
    Wait 30 seconds,
    Exit 0.
If 'the doomed child' is greater than 0 then,
    Send signal 9 to process 'the doomed child',
    a number called 'the reaped corpse' is reap any child process,
    a number called 'how the child died' is the reaped status,
    Print "a real exit code: {'exit code of' of 'how the child died'}",
    Print "a real signal: {'signal of' of 'how the child died'}",
    Print "really crashed: {crashed of 'how the child died'}",
    Print "really exited normally: {'exited normally' of 'how the child died'}".
